#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228877 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d35c9f72-a876-5fc0-8ab4-741c37cb3775" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Subsequent Percentages as a Single Percentage"}] :question/id #uuid "4f528740-2fa6-5441-ab8b-86ce294dbf75" :question/math-academy-id "e-21447" :question/problem "According to a recent report, $40\\%$ of viewers said they enjoyed documentaries. Of those, $50\\%$ preferred documentaries about nature. What percentage of all viewers preferred nature documentaries?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the total number of viewers.\n\nWe're told that $40\\%$ of viewers enjoyed documentaries. Thus, the number of viewers who enjoy documentaries is\n$0.4x$.\n\nWe are also told that of those viewers who enjoy documentaries, $50\\%$ preferred nature topics. So, the number of viewers who preferred nature documentaries is\n\n$$\n0.50(0.4x) = 0.20x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.20x$ represents $20\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $20\\%$ of all viewers preferred nature documentaries."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228882 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3852a0f9-f1ac-5a3f-9883-8dd2ee03e332" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Subsequent Percentages in Real-World Situations"}] :question/id #uuid "97d0ab51-97cd-5781-be30-4d70adc5d214" :question/math-academy-id "e-21458" :question/problem "A service adjusted a subscription fee to $90\\%$ of its original cost. A surcharge of $18\\%$ was applied to the adjusted fee. If the subscription originally cost $\\[math]180$, what is the surcharge?" :question/worked-solution "First, the adjusted fee is $90\\%$ of the original cost $(\\[math]180){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90% \\cdot 180 &= \\frac{90}{100} \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 0.9 \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 162\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the adjusted fee is $\\[/math]162$. Then, the surcharge is $18\\%$ of this fee:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18% \\cdot 162 &= \\frac{18}{100} \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 0.18 \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 29.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surcharge is $\\[math]29.16$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec0d62dd-5b51-5286-b382-776412570561" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Subsequent Percentages"}] :question/id #uuid "91ca6940-1bc7-583b-8bac-81d7da934167" :question/math-academy-id "e-21463" :question/problem "Find $160\\%$ of $75$, and then $150\\%$ of the result." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $160\\%$ of $75{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n160% \\cdot 75 &= \\frac{160}{100} \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 1.6 \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $150\\%$ of the result $(120){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n150% \\cdot 120 &= \\frac{150}{100} \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 1.5 \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 180\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $180$."}]}
